course: biology
lesson: 174
title: Reef giants and wanderers: whale sharks, mantas, dugongs and sea turtles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some reef visitors are giants: the whale shark is a fish as long as a bus, yet it swims slowly with its huge mouth open and eats only tiny drifting food, like a gentle bus-sized vacuum cleaner.
explain: Manta rays fly through the water on wings wider than a car is long and sometimes leap right out of the sea, while dugongs graze on sea grass like underwater cows.
explain: Sea turtles are the great wanderers: a baby crawls from its sand nest to the sea at night, swims thousands of kilometres for years, and many females come back to lay eggs on the very beach where they hatched.
explain: To a turtle, a plastic bag drifting in the water can look just like a jellyfish, which is one reason rubbish in the sea is so dangerous.
fact: The whale shark is the largest fish in the world, about as long as a bus; it eats tiny drifting food, and each one has a pattern of spots as unique as a fingerprint.
fact: Manta rays are huge rays with wings up to about seven metres across, and they have the biggest brains for their body size of any fish.
fact: Dugongs are sea cows that graze on sea grass, and sailors long ago may have mistaken dugongs and manatees for mermaids.
fact: Sea turtle babies hatch from eggs buried in sand and head for the sea, and warmer sand makes more of the babies female.
fact: Sea turtles travel thousands of kilometres across oceans, often riding ocean currents, and many females return to lay eggs on the beach where they hatched.
fact: Sea turtles sometimes eat plastic bags, mistaking them for jellyfish; leatherback turtles mostly eat jellyfish and have spines in their throats to hold them.
fact: Some reef sharks can rest on the sea floor and still breathe, and sharks have been around for hundreds of millions of years, longer than trees.
fact: The coconut crab is the largest land crab in the world, it can climb palm trees, and its pinch is one of the strongest of any animal.
fact: A big coconut crab is about a metre across with its legs spread, so it would still fit in your arms; like other crabs it has ten legs, counting its two big claws.
fact: Hammerhead sharks have wide heads with an eye at each end, and they swing their heads from side to side as they search; some gather in big schools near islands.
fact: Hawksbill turtles eat sponges that many other animals cannot eat, and this helps corals have more space to grow.
fact: Remoras stick to sharks and rays with a sucker on their heads, and that sucker is really a changed fin.
exercise: What is the largest fish in the world? | The whale shark | The blue whale | The manta ray | The great white shark
exercise: What does a whale shark eat? | Tiny drifting food | Other smaller sharks | Divers who swim too close | Only big fish and seals
exercise: How do scientists tell one whale shark from another? | By its pattern of spots, unique like a fingerprint | By its colour, since each one is a different shade of blue | By its song | By counting all its teeth
exercise: How wide can a manta ray's wings be? | About seven metres | About seven centimetres | About seventy metres | About one metre long
exercise: Which fish have the biggest brains for their body size? | Manta rays | Goldfish | Whale sharks | Sardines
exercise: What do dugongs eat? | Sea grass | Small fish | Jellyfish | Coral
exercise: Which animals might sailors long ago have mistaken for mermaids? | Dugongs and manatees | Sea turtles | Whale sharks and manta rays | Dolphins only
exercise: What decides whether baby sea turtles become males or females? | The temperature of the sand | The colour of the moon on the night of laying | The size of the mother | The taste of the water
exercise: Where do many female sea turtles lay their eggs? | On the beach where they hatched | On floating seaweed | In coral caves | On any beach at all they happen to find first
exercise: Why do sea turtles sometimes eat plastic bags? | The bags look like jellyfish | They like the taste of plastic | They use them to build their nests | They eat them by accident while sleeping
exercise: What do leatherback turtles mostly eat? | Jellyfish | Sea grass | Sponges | Crabs
exercise: Which came first on Earth? | Sharks, long before trees | Trees, long before sharks | Both came at the same time | Sharks came after people
exercise: What is the coconut crab? | The largest land crab in the world | A crab made of coconut shell | A crab that lives only in deep water | A crab the size of a coin
exercise: What is special about a hammerhead shark's head? | It is wide with an eye at each end | It has no eyes at all | It has three eyes | It is round like a ball with eyes all around
exercise: What does a hawksbill turtle eat that many animals cannot? | Sponges | Coral rock | Sea grass | Plankton
exercise: How does a remora ride on a shark? | It sticks on with a sucker that is a changed fin | It holds on tightly with its teeth | It sits on the shark's back with little legs | It is glued on by the shark
test: In Finding Nemo, Crush the sea turtle surfs a big ocean current. Which part is true? | Real sea turtles ride ocean currents to travel thousands of kilometres | Real turtles never leave one small beach | Real turtles can only swim in rivers | Real turtles talk like surfers
test: In Moana, Tamatoa is a giant crab bigger than a house. What is real? | The coconut crab is the largest land crab, but it fits in your arms | Coconut crabs are bigger than houses | Coconut crabs live only in the deepest sea trenches of the world | There are no real land crabs
test: «a coconut crab and 2 turtles, how many legs n flippers» | 18 | 14 | 12 | 16
test: «4 hammerheads, how many eyes» | 8 | 4 | 12 | 16
test: «a shark as long as a bus is coming!!! get out» What is right? | If it is a whale shark, it eats tiny drifting food and is not hunting people | All big sharks eat people | It must be a blue whale | Whale sharks eat boats
test: What would happen if a beach where turtles nest got much hotter? | More of the babies hatching there would be female | All the babies hatching there would be male, every single one | The eggs would hatch into crabs | Nothing would change
test: «threw my crisp bag in the sea, it's just a bag mate» Why is that harmful? | A turtle may swallow it, thinking it is a jellyfish | Bags turn into fish | The sea melts plastic in a day | Turtles use bags as houses
test: «sharks have to keep swimming or they die, all of them» What is right? | Not all: some reef sharks can rest on the sea floor and still breathe | Yes, every shark dies if it stops | Yes, sharks never sleep at all | No, sharks breathe air at the surface
test: A diver sees a small fish fixed to a manta's belly, upside down. What is it? | A remora holding on with its sucker | A baby manta riding along on its back | A big barnacle stuck tight to its skin | A fish that the manta caught and is carrying
test: «dugongs are basically mermaids lol» What is right? | They are sea cows that graze sea grass, and sailors may once have mistaken them for mermaids | Yes, they are half fish and half people | They are a kind of shark | They are giant sea turtles
test: Why might a reef have more room for corals where hawksbill turtles live? | Hawksbills eat sponges that would otherwise crowd the corals | Hawksbills plant new corals | Hawksbills eat all the fish | Hawksbills clean the water by filtering it through their big shells
exam: Identify the largest extant species of fish. | The whale shark | The blue whale of the open oceans | The giant manta ray | The great white shark
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the determination of sex in the offspring of sea turtles? | It is governed by the temperature of the sand in which the eggs are incubated | It is determined by the phase of the moon at laying | It is determined by the age of the mother | It is fixed by the salinity of the adjacent sea
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which sea turtles are observed to ingest plastic bags. | The bags are mistaken for jellyfish, upon which certain turtles feed | Plastic is a necessary component of their diet | The bags are employed in the construction of nests | Ingestion occurs exclusively during sleep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sucker of the remora. | It is a modified fin by which the fish attaches itself to sharks and rays | It is an adhesive gland secreting a glue | It is a set of hooked teeth | It is an organ borrowed from the host shark and retained throughout the life of the fish
